# Things about YOU that must never appear in a file this repo ships.
#
# This file is the example. Copy it to .private-patterns, which is gitignored,
# and put your real values there. `make check-private` reads them and fails if
# any of them turns up in a tracked file, a staged change, or (with --history)
# a commit message.
#
# You do not need to list credentials: every value in .env is picked up
# automatically, and so are the personal leaves of your config.yaml (teachers,
# rooms, SSID, IP prefix, campus coordinates, notebook and folder names) and
# your git commit e-mail.
#
# What belongs here is everything else that identifies you or somebody near
# you. One entry per line. A line wrapped in slashes is a regular expression,
# matched case-insensitively; anything else is a plain substring.
#
#   Your legal name, and any short form of it you actually use
#   Your e-mail addresses, including the ones not in .env
#   Your macOS short username, and the name your Mac broadcasts on a network
#   Your student number, employee number, or any other file number
#   The name of anyone whose device pairs with yours: a partner, a housemate
#   Your home Wi-Fi name, and the coordinates of anywhere you sleep
#   A bundle identifier or URL scheme built out of your own name
#
# Example entries, all fictional:
#
#   Jordan Tremblay
#   jtremblay
#   jordan.tremblay@example.com
#   /\b1234567\b/
#   Jordans-MacBook
#   /45\.51[0-9]{2}/
